The two parental inbred strains provide the fixed haplotypes 00000 and 11111. Each meiosis imposes exactly one crossover in one of the four intervals between the five SNPs, and the single permitted orientation plus positive-strand restriction means each meiosis produces exactly one of the two reciprocal recombinant products.

Consequently every F2 chromosome is one of the eight single-transition strings. Every F3 chromosome is then produced by one additional crossover performed on a pair of such strings. The resulting haplotype therefore contains at most two transitions, and exhaustive enumeration shows that every binary string of length 5 that possesses 0, 1 or 2 transitions is attainable:

- 0 transitions: the two parental strings (recovered when the second crossover cancels the transitions already present on the F2 pair);
- 1 transition: the eight strings already enumerated;
- 2 transitions: \(\binom{4}{2}\times 2=12\) strings.

No string with three or more transitions can arise under the two-crossover limit, and every one of the 22 strings listed above is realized by at least one admissible F2 pair plus crossover placement. Hence exactly 22 distinct sequences are possible on the arbitrarily chosen positive strand in the F3 generation.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {22}

