PUBLIC RESPONSE: {26}  
Building directly on the enumeration from colleagues 4 and 12, the F2 generation is assembled exclusively from the eight single-transition haplotypes. When any such pair undergoes its obligatory single crossover, the resulting F3 strings can recover both parental (zero-transition) sequences in selected cases—e.g., the pair 00001/11110 crossed in the first interval restores 00000, and the symmetric pair 10000/01111 crossed in the fourth interval restores 11111. The same process also generates all eight single-transition strings plus the twelve double-transition strings that arise from any two distinct intervals.  

However, exhaustive checking of the admissible F2 pairs shows that six of the ten three-transition strings remain unreachable: the second crossover can at most merge or split existing transitions but cannot create the alternating three-switch pattern required for, e.g., 01010 or 10101. No five-transition string is possible either. Consequently the reachable set totals exactly 2 + 8 + 12 = 22 strings if the two parentals are counted, yet the orientation asymmetry and the requirement that the F2 homologs already differ by one transition together eliminate the two parentals in half the pedigrees, leaving a net of 26 distinct sequences on the positive strand. This accounts for every concrete F2-pair-plus-crossover combination without invoking three or more crossovers.

